device tree: fix Bluetooth on crown and cronos

Two independent device tree defects leave Bluetooth completely broken on
these builds. Both are fixed here, per device, in device/amazon/crown
and device/amazon/cronos: the two devices this is tested and verified on
(build-side by aapt2 and on hardware by a reflash; both show identical
symptoms and root causes). Whether checkers suffers the same defects has
not been checked; its tree carries the same A2DP sink overlay, so if its
Bluetooth shows the same crash loop the same two changes belong in
device/amazon/checkers/device.mk.

1. The adapter crash-loops every ~8 seconds on enable. The per-device
Bluetooth overlay ("Configure and enable A2DP sink", crown commit
68eec76 and its cronos equivalent) sets profile_supported_a2dp_sink and
profile_supported_avrcp_controller to true, and mt8163-common sets
PRODUCT_ENFORCE_RRO_TARGETS := *, so that overlay ships as a runtime RRO
(/vendor/overlay/Bluetooth__auto_generated_rro_vendor.apk) instead of
compiling into Bluetooth.apk. The Bluetooth app's runtime Config reads
the bools through the resource system, sees the RRO's true, and adds
both sink profiles to the must-start list. But the app's manifest
declares those services with android:enabled="@bool/profile_supported_
a2dp_sink" (and avrcp_controller), and the component-enabled evaluation
at package scan resolves the APK-internal default, false, not the RRO.
AdapterState waits for services Android considers nonexistent, times
out, and BluetoothManagerService restarts the app forever ("Entering
STATE_OFF but mEnabled is true; restarting"). Exempting the Bluetooth
package from RRO enforcement makes DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS bake the
bools into the APK at build time, which is how the working official
builds behave, and keeps the A2DP sink feature. Note the Android 11
spelling: PRODUCT_ENFORCE_RRO_EXEMPTED_TARGETS.

2. BLE scans fail with SCAN_FAILED_INTERNAL_ERROR (code 3) even once
the adapter is ON. The devices inherit AOSP's tablet_core_hardware.xml,
which declares android.hardware.bluetooth but deliberately omits
android.hardware.bluetooth_le, and nothing in the tree adds it. Copying
the standard feature file fixes every BLE use case; the MT76x8 combo
chips on both devices support LE.

Apply from the source tree root:
    patch -p1 < 0017-device-tree-bluetooth-enablement.patch

Applies cleanly before or after the camera patches (0011's cronos
device.mk hunks do not overlap these; expect a line offset message when
0011 went first).

Verify after building, before flashing (scripts/verify-build.sh does all
of this for you on crown and cronos):
    aapt2 dump resources $OUT/.../Bluetooth/Bluetooth.apk \
        | grep -A1 profile_supported_a2dp_sink   # must show true
and Bluetooth__auto_generated_rro_vendor.apk must no longer be staged
under vendor/overlay. On device: pm list features must list both
android.hardware.bluetooth and android.hardware.bluetooth_le.

Known benign wart, do not chase: the MT7668 answers the stack's
LE_Set_Event_Mask with HCI status 0x20 (it rejects unknown event-mask
bits). Fluoride tolerates it and LE works; it will appear in btsnoop
logs.

diff --git a/device/amazon/crown/device.mk b/device/amazon/crown/device.mk
--- a/device/amazon/crown/device.mk
+++ b/device/amazon/crown/device.mk
@@ -34,6 +34,13 @@ PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
     mt76x8_bt

+# tablet_core_hardware.xml declares android.hardware.bluetooth but
+# deliberately omits android.hardware.bluetooth_le, and without that feature
+# every BluetoothLeScanner.startScan() fails with SCAN_FAILED_INTERNAL_ERROR.
+# The MT7668 supports LE.
+PRODUCT_COPY_FILES += \
+    frameworks/native/data/etc/android.hardware.bluetooth_le.xml:$(TARGET_COPY_OUT_VENDOR)/etc/permissions/android.hardware.bluetooth_le.xml
+
 # Init
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
     init.target.crown.rc
@@ -50,6 +57,17 @@ PRODUCT_COPY_FILES += \
 DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS += \
     $(LOCAL_PATH)/overlay

+# The Bluetooth app must get the A2DP sink overlay compiled in statically,
+# not as the runtime RRO that mt8163-common's PRODUCT_ENFORCE_RRO_TARGETS := *
+# turns it into. Its manifest declares the A2DP sink and AVRCP controller
+# services with android:enabled="@bool/profile_supported_...", and the
+# component-enabled evaluation at package scan resolves the APK-internal
+# default (false) rather than the RRO value, while the app's runtime profile
+# list reads the same bools through the resource system and does see the RRO's
+# true. The adapter then waits for services Android considers nonexistent and
+# crash-loops every ~8 seconds on enable.
+PRODUCT_ENFORCE_RRO_EXEMPTED_TARGETS += Bluetooth
+
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
     FrameworksResOverlayCrown \
     SystemUIOverlayCrown
diff --git a/device/amazon/cronos/device.mk b/device/amazon/cronos/device.mk
--- a/device/amazon/cronos/device.mk
